{"kind":"report","text":"Review of Task 2e fresh-session orientation at 359911001fbcdea9c1317485865b1ceb1a5b4f15: CHANGES-REQUESTED. Disclosure: the bulk of this pass was performed by a delegated reviewer working to my instructions; I verified the load-bearing claims of both blockers myself before signing, and say below which. Head verified exact, worktree clean before and after, all probes deleted; vet, test, build and race on both changed packages exit zero. The headline performance claim is real and if anything understated, which makes the blockers worth fixing rather than the feature worth abandoning. F1, blocker: the resident's answer overrides local durable truth. whoami accepts an orientation when genesis, head and fingerprint match, then returns the resident's Durable payload — name, kind, roles, membership event, role sources — and its Depth verbatim, labelled resident_verified with no degraded flag. The fingerprint check cannot fail, and I confirmed this in two halves and then live: handleOrientation sets Fingerprint from the caller-supplied path value and echoes it, while the client compares that echo against its own, so the equality is trivially true. Against a running service I requested orientation for not-a-real-fingerprint and received HTTP 200 with that string echoed back. The delegate demonstrated the consequence: a resident agreeing on genesis and head returned roles operator, ratifier and sequencer against a local fold of participant, a different kind, a fabricated membership event and a depth of 999999, and whoami reported the resident's answer as verified. The realistic trigger is not malice but version skew — gs serve is long-lived while gitseq-mcp is spawned per session, ActorState derivation changed three times in this branch's own recent history, and Orientation carries no fold or projection version, so an older resident at a matching head yields a different answer presented as verified. Two aggravating facts: gitseq-mcp --server applies no