 F2 lives are never executed, and there is no cold arm, so it neither produces nor supports the cited numbers. Re-measured like for like against a real warmed server over a real 274-deep log with a genuinely cold client, the claim holds: 26 milliseconds on the resident path against 20.647 seconds for the full audit. Replace the benchmark with both arms against a real server at two or three actual depths. What held is worth recording, because the fallback path is genuinely solid: a cross-genesis resident at a matching head is rejected, a resident genuinely behind the local head is rejected, and eleven malformed or hostile response shapes — 404 in two flavours, empty body, HTML, truncated JSON, wrong types, redirect loop, and a resident that accepts then never answers — all fall back cleanly with the hung case bounded at 1.019 seconds, so condition four is fully met. Protocol compatibility is unbroken in both eras and the new fields are purely additive. Orientation leaks no session identifier, key material or path, though note that /v0/actors filters to locally-held custody while /v0/orientation does not. Cache invalidation is sound; the failure mode is the opposite one. Approval is withheld for this exact head only and I have not merged anything.","body":{"delegation":"bulk of the pass performed by a delegated reviewer to my instructions; disclosed in the report","findings":"2 blockers (resident payload overrides local durable truth behind a vacuous fingerprint check; cold resident can never serve orientation within the 1s budget), 3 lesser (degraded false-positives on frontier movement, unbounded response decode, benchmark measures neither depth nor the real server)","head":"359911001fbcdea9c1317485865b1ceb1a5b4f15","result":"changes-requested","verified_by_me":"head and cleanliness; the vacuous fingerprint check confirmed by reading both halves and then live against a running service returning HTTP 200 for a nonexistent fingerprint; the 1s timeout and absence of a 